ecreases. So the monotonicity claim is false in general. The solution's lower bound argument relies on monotonicity to claim that each column left of the path yields a contiguous block of rows that belong to left region, etc. If sigma is not monotone, the left region might be more complicated. However, they could choose a different monotone path: maybe they define the path as the \"upper envelope\" of the points? Let's examine the path definition more carefully.\n\nThey say: \"Join successive points (i, sigma(i)) and (i+1, sigma(i+1)) by a monotone lattice path consisting of a horizontal segment followed (if necessary) by a vertical segment; the direction of the horizontal segment is to the right, the direction of the vertical segment is upward.\" So for each step from i to i+1, they go horizontally from column sigma(i) to column sigma(i+1)? Actually, they start at (i, sigma(i)), then go horizontally to (i+1, sigma(i)), then vertical to (i+1, sigma(i+1)). That is, they move right one unit (increase row index) while staying at same column sigma(i), then move up or down to sigma(i+1). But they say vertical direction is upward, so they assume sigma(i+1) >= sigma(i). If sigma(i+1) < sigma(i), they'd need to go downwards, which they disallow. So they implicitly assume sigma is monotone increasing. But sigma is a permutation, not necessarily monotone. So the construction of the path as described fails for arbitrary sigma.\n\nBut perhaps they consider a different path: they could define a monotone path that goes from (1, sigma(1)) to (n, sigma(n)) by connecting each point to the next via a path that goes right then up, but if sigma(i+1) < sigma(i), they could go up first then right? Actually, they could go up first then right to ensure monotonicity: from (i, sigma(i)) go up to (i, sigma(i+1)) then right to (i+1, sigma(i+1)). But they specifically said horizontal then vertical. So it's ambiguous.\n\nNevertheless, the monotonicity of the path is crucial for the argument that