nd the explicit construction match, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe label the rows and columns of the grid by the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) let the unique uncovered unit square be at column \\(p(i)\\); thus the set  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nis a permutation matrix (the function \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a bijection).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) the square \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) is the **left‑corner** of that row: it is the rightmost covered square to the left of the uncovered one.  \nSimilarly, for a row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) the square \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) is the **right‑corner** of that row.\n\nBecause the numbers \\(p(i)\\) are all distinct, the columns \\(p(i)-1\\) (for those rows with \\(p(i)>1\\)) are also all distinct.  Hence there are exactly \\(n-1\\) left‑corners (all rows except the unique row with \\(p(i)=1\\)) and exactly \\(n-1\\) right‑corners (all rows except the unique row with \\(p(i)=n\\)).\n\nConsider any rectangular tile \\(T\\).  By definition its interior consists of unit squares all lying on the same side of the uncovered square of each row it meets; consequently:\n\n* \\(T\\) cannot contain two left‑corners, because two left‑corners lie in different columns.\n* \\(T\\) cannot contain a left‑corner together with a right‑corner, for a left‑corner lies to the left of the uncovered square of its row while a right‑corner lies to its right, and a rectangle cannot straddle the uncovered square.\n\nThus each tile can cover **at most one** left‑corner and **at most one** right‑corner, and a tile covering a left‑corner cannot cover any right‑corner.  Consequently we need at least one tile for each left‑corner and one tile for each right‑corner.  Hence an