st one tile for each left\u2011corner and one tile for each right\u2011corner.  Hence any admissible tiling uses at least\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.  For \\(n=2025\\) this lower bound equals \\(4048\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*For the squares to the right of the diagonal* (those with column index \\(j>i\\)) place, for each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\), a horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).\n\n*For the squares to the left of the diagonal* (those with column index \\(j<i\\)) place, for each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\), a vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).\n\n**Verification that the tiles are admissible and disjoint**\n\n* Each \\(H_i\\) lies entirely in row \\(i\\) and uses only columns \\(>i\\); therefore it never contains the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\).  \n* Each \\(V_j\\) lies entirely in column \\(j\\) and uses only rows \\(>j\\); therefore it never contains the uncovered square \\((j,j)\\).  \n* A horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) and a vertical tile \\(V_j\\) intersect only at the square \\((i,j)\\).  If \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(H_i\\) but not to \\(V_j\\) (because \\(V_j\\) contains rows \\(>j\\) only).  If \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(V_j\\) but not to \\(H_i\\) (because \\(H_i\\) uses columns \\(>i\\) only).  Hence no two tiles share a unit square.\n\nConsequently the collection \\(\\{H_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\) is a family of pairwise disjoint rectangles covering every unit square except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  The number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n| \\{H_i\\}|+|\