trix.  Hence there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\nFor a column \\(j\\) define  \n\n\\[\nr_j=\\sigma^{-1}(j)\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n),\n\\]\n\nthe row containing the hole of column \\(j\\).  Column \\(j\\) is therefore partitioned into two (possibly empty) intervals  \n\n\\[\nT_j=\\{(i,j):1\\le i<r_j\\},\\qquad   \nB_j=\\{(i,j):r_j<i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll squares of \\(T_j\\) and \\(B_j\\) must be covered; the square \\((r_j,j)\\) is left uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Structure of a tile)\n\nLet a tile be a rectangle whose set of rows is a contiguous interval\n\\(I=[a,b]\\) and whose set of columns is a contiguous interval\n\\(J=[c,d]\\).  \nBecause a tile cannot contain a hole, for every column \\(j\\in J\\) we have either\n\\(I\\subseteq T_j\\) or \\(I\\subseteq B_j\\).  \nMoreover the choice “top’’ or “bottom’’ is the same for all columns of the tile; otherwise the rectangle would contain the hole of a column where the two choices differ.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf for some \\(j\\in J\\) the interval \\(I\\) intersected both \\(T_j\\) and \\(B_j\\), then the row \\(r_j\\) would lie between \\(a\\) and \\(b\\); consequently the square \\((r_j,j)\\) (the hole) would belong to the rectangle, contradicting the hypothesis. ∎\n\nConsequently every tile is either a **top tile** (its rows lie entirely in the top intervals of all its columns) or a **bottom tile** (its rows lie entirely in the bottom intervals of all its columns).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (At least \\(n-1\\) top tiles)\n\nOrder the columns so that  \n\n\\[\nr_{1}<r_{2}<\\dots <r_{n}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\{r_j\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), we have \\(r_{1}=1\\) and \\(r_{n}=n\\).\n\nFor each \\(k\\ge 2\\) consider the cell  \n\n\\[\np_k=(r_{k}-1,\\;k).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(r_{k}>1\\), the cell \\(p_k\\) belongs to the top interval \\(T_{k}\\).  \nSince \\(r_{k-1}<r_{k}\\), we have \\(r_{k-1}\\le r_{k}-1\\); thus the row of \\(p_k\\) is not less than \\(r_{k-1}\\), and consequently \\(p_k\\notin T_{k-1}\\).\n\nLet a top tile cover \\(p_k\\).  By Lemma 1 its row interval \\(I\\) must satisfy \\(I\\subseteq T_{k}\\).  If the tile also contained column \\(k-1\\), then we would have \\(I\\subseteq T_{k-1}\\) as well, which is impossible because \\(p_k\\notin T_{k-1}\\).  Hence the top tile covering \\(p_k\\) cannot contain column \\(k-1\\).\n\nMoreover a top tile can cover at most one of the cells \\(p_2,p_3,\\dots ,p_n\\): if a top tile covered both \\(p_k\\) and \\(p_{k'}\\) with \\(k<k'\\), then its row interval would have to contain both rows \\(r_k-1\\) and \\(r_{k'}-1\\).  Since \\(r_{k'}-1\\ge r_k\\), the interval would contain a row \\(\\ge r_k\\), contradicting the requirement \\(I\\subseteq T_k\\) (which consists only of rows \\(<r_k\\)).  Therefore each top tile can accommodate at most one of the cells \\(p_k\\).\n\nThus we need at least one distinct top tile for each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\), i.e. at least \\(n-1\\) top tiles. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (At least \\(n-1\\) bottom tiles)\n\nApply Lemma 2 to the transposed board (swap rows and columns).  The condition “each row and each column has exactly one uncovered square’’ is invariant under transposition, and top intervals become left intervals while bottom intervals become right intervals.  Consequently the transposed board requires at least \\(n-1\\) top tiles, which correspond to at least \\(n-1\\) bottom tiles in the original orientation. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound\n\nFrom Lemmas 2 and 3 any tiling of the board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) place  \n\n* a vertical tile covering all squares of \\(T_j\\) (if \\(T_j\\neq\\varnothing\\));\n* a vertical tile covering all squares of \\(B_j\\) (if \\(B_j\\neq\\varnothing\\)).\n\nThese tiles are disjoint because each occupies a distinct column.  Exactly one column has its hole in the top row (\\(r_j=1\\)), so that column contributes no top tile; exactly one column has its hole in the bottom row (\\(r_j=n\\)), so that column contributes no bottom tile.  All other columns contribute two tiles.  Hence the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n-2.\n\\]\n\nEvery square not belonging to a hole is covered, each tile’s sides lie on grid lines, and each row and column contains precisely one uncovered square (the hole).  Thus the construction satisfies all requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nBoth the lower bound and the explicit construction give the same number \\(2n-2\\).  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\text{ tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
