i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\) (size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\)).  \n   - For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a vertical tile covering \\((j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\) (size \\((n-j)\\times1\\)).  \n   - These tiles are pairwise disjoint, cover every non\u2011diagonal square, and leave exactly the diagonal squares uncovered.  \n   - Number of tiles: \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).  \n\n6. **Conclusion.** The lower bound and the construction match, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the rows and columns of the grid by the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a bijection (a permutation).  In row \\(i\\) the uncovered square lies in column \\(p(i)\\); in column \\(j\\) the uncovered square lies in row \\(p^{-1}(j)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Left\u2011corner and right\u2011corner squares  \n\nFor a row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) define the *left\u2011corner*\n\\[\nL_i=(i,p(i)-1).\n\\]\nFor a row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) define the *right\u2011corner*\n\\[\nR_i=(i,p(i)+1).\n\\]\n\nSince the values \\(p(i)\\) are all distinct, the columns \\(p(i)-1\\) (for those rows with \\(p(i)>1\\)) are also all distinct; consequently there are exactly \\(n-1\\) left\u2011corners.  Similarly there are exactly \\(n-1\\) right\u2011corners.\n\nEvery unit square other than those in \\(U\\) must be covered by a tile, so each left\u2011corner and each right\u2011corner must belong to some tile.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lemma on the capacity of a tile  \n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet \\(T\\) be a rectangular tile.  Then\n\n1. \\(T\\) contains at most one left\u2011corner;\n2. \\(T\\) contains at most one right\u2011corner;\n3. \\(T\\) cannot contain a left\u2011corner and a right\u2011corner simultaneously.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause the sides of a tile lie on grid lines, the set of columns occupied by \\(T\\) is an interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) (with \\(c_1\\le c_2\\)), and the set of rows occupied by \\(T\\) is an interval \\([r_1,r_2]\\).\n\nSuppose \\(T\\) contains a left\u2011corner \\(L_i=(i,p(i)-1)\\).  Then column \\(p(i)-1\\) belongs to \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  If \\(c_2\\ge p(i)\\), the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\) would also lie in \\(T\\), contradicting the requirement that uncovered squares are not covered.  Hence\n\\[\nc_2\\le p(i)-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsider any other row \\(j\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\).  The uncovered square in that row is \\((j,p(j))\\).  Because \\(c_2\\le p(i)-1\\), condition (1) forces \\(p(j)>c_2\\); otherwise \\((j,p(j))\\) would lie in column \\(\\le c_2\\) and be covered by \\(T\\).  Thus\n\\[\np(j)\\ge p(i). \\tag{2}\n\\]\nSince \\(p\\) is a permutation, \\(p(j)=p(i)\\) can occur only when \\(j=i\\); for \\(j\\neq i\\) we have \\(p(j)>p(i)\\).  Consequently the left\u2011corner of row \\(j\\) is at column \\(p(j)-1>p(i)-1\\ge c_2\\), which lies **outside** the column interval of \\(T\\).  Hence no other left\u2011corner belongs to \\(T\\).  This proves (1).\n\nThe argument for right\u2011corners is symmetric: if \\(T\\) contains a right\u2011corner \\(R_i=(i,p(i)+1)\\) then its leftmost column satisfies \\(c_1\\ge p(i)+1\\), and any other row covered by \\(T\\) must have its uncovered column \\(<p(i)\\), preventing that row\u2019s right\u2011corner from lying inside \\(T\\).  Thus at most one right\u2011corner can belong to \\(T\\).\n\nFinally, a tile cannot contain both a left\u2011corner and a right\u2011corner, because a left\u2011corner forces the rightmost column of the tile to be \\(\\le p(i)-1\\) while a right\u2011corner forces the leftmost column to be \\(\\ge p(k)+1\\); these two inequalities are incompatible.\n\n\u220e\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEvery left\u2011corner and every right\u2011corner must be covered, and by the Lemma each tile can cover at most one left\u2011corner and at most one right\u2011corner, never both.  Therefore any tiling requires at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.  For \\(n=2025\\) this yields the lower bound \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  Then the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal squares\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the following tiles:\n\n*For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)*, let\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\na horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).\n\n*For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\)*, let\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\na vertical rectangle of size \\((n-j)\\times1\\).\n\n**Verification.**\n\n1. **Coverage.**  \n   - If a square \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(j>i\\), then \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\).  \n   - If \\(j<i\\), then \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\).  \n   - The only squares not belonging to any \\(H_i\\) or \\(V_j\\) are those with \\(j=i\\), i.e. the diagonal squares \\(D\\).  Hence every non\u2011diagonal square is covered exactly once.\n\n2. **Disjointness.**  \n   - Distinct horizontal tiles lie in distinct rows, so they are disjoint.  \n   - Distinct vertical tiles lie in distinct columns, so they are disjoint.  \n   - A horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) occupies columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\); a vertical tile \\(V_j\\) occupies rows \\(j+1,\\dots ,n\\).  Their potential intersection would be the square \\((i,j)\\).  For such an intersection we would need simultaneously \\(j\\ge i+1\\) (so that \\((i,j)\\) lies in \\(H_i\\)) and \\(i\\ge j+1\\) (so that \\((i,j)\\) lies in \\(V_j\\)), which is impossible.  Thus no horizontal tile meets any vertical tile.\n\n3. **Number of tiles.**  \n   - There are \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\).  \n   - There are \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles \\(V_2,\\dots ,V_n\\).  \n   - Total tiles \\(=2n-2\\).\n\nThus we have exhibited a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles that satisfies the required condition.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles proved in Section\u202f4 cannot be improved, and the construction of Section\u202f5 attains this bound.  Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given grid with \\(n=2025\\) we obtain\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing the output, I have carefully reviewed the **Summary** and **Detailed Solution** sections to ensure:\n\n* All mathematical statements are expressed in TeX.\n* The final answer appears in `\\boxed{}` format.\n* The lower\u2011bound argument is fully justified by the Lemma, with a clear proof that each tile can contain at most one left\u2011corner and at most one right\u2011corner, and cannot contain both.\n* The construction is described explicitly, and a point\u2011by\u2011point verification shows that every non\u2011diagonal square is covered exactly once while the diagonal squares remain uncovered.\n* No extraneous commentary or incomplete reasoning remains.\n\nThe solution now conforms to all the requirements of the system prompt."
